# Line endings are LF in the repository and LF in the working tree, on every
# platform.
#
# Not a style preference. Without this, `core.autocrlf=true` — the default a
# Windows Git install sets up — checks every text file out with CRLF, and
# `scripts/*.test.mjs` then fails to parse: Vitest reports
# `SyntaxError: Invalid or unexpected token` and collects zero tests from the
# file. `node --check` on the same file passes, which is what makes it worth a
# comment rather than a line.
#
# The shape of the failure is the reason it went unnoticed: CI runs on
# ubuntu-latest, where the checkout is LF and everything is green, while the
# developer who wrote the tests sees two red files the moment they check out a
# branch instead of writing it. A gate that is green for the machine and red for
# the person is the same defect as one that is green for the wrong reason.
#
# `text=auto` still lets Git decide what is text and what is binary; `eol=lf`
# fixes what happens to the ones it decides are text.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary, so Git does not go looking for lines in them at all.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.pdf binary
